    <string name="backup_start">Crear copia de seguridad</string>
    <!--
      The consequence a deterministic file name has and nothing else on the sheet names: an archive is
      <package>-<versionCode>.thorbak, so backing the same app up twice at the same version always
      collides.

      This used to read "can replace the backup you already have", because the three destinations
      genuinely disagreed: MediaStore de-duplicates to "name (1)", a SAF provider usually does, and a
      legacy Downloads write renamed straight over the old file. AppArchiveStoreImpl now picks a
      non-colliding name on that third backend too (nonCollidingArchiveName), so "kept alongside" is
      the behaviour Thor itself produces everywhere.

      The hedge survives, narrowed to the one case Thor does not control: a folder the user chose is
      the provider's to manage, and DocumentsContract.renameDocument onto an existing name is the
      provider's decision. Promising "never replaces" would be a claim Thor cannot honour there.

      Stated before the button rather than as an "are you sure?", which is the branch's rule for
      destructive actions: name the consequence, do not ask the user to confirm a sentence they have
      not been given.
    -->
    <string name="backup_overwrite_notice">Thor nombra cada copia de seguridad según la aplicación y su versión, así que volver a copiar esta aplicación en la misma versión reutiliza ese nombre. Thor conserva las dos numerando la más reciente, aunque una carpeta que hayas elegido tú puede sustituir el archivo anterior en su lugar.</string>
    <!--
      Shown when every data class is unticked. Its second sentence is the whole of C1: the restore gate
      refuses an empty selection before it looks at the installer, so an installer-only archive is a
      file Thor can write and can never read back. The sheet used to offer exactly that.
    -->
    <string name="backup_nothing_selected">Selecciona al menos un elemento para la copia de seguridad. El instalador por sí solo no se puede restaurar.</string>
    <string name="backup_done">Copia de seguridad guardada.</string>
    <string name="backup_failed">Error en la copia de seguridad: %1$s</string>
    <string name="backup_failed_unknown">se detuvo sin decir por qué</string>
    <!--
      The backup counterpart of restore_failed_partial, and deliberately not a copy of it: a backup
      reads the app's data and writes somewhere else, so there is no half-written app to warn about.
      What the user needs is the other half — that no archive was left behind either. That is true by
      construction rather than by hope: AppArchiveStoreImpl writes to <name>.part and only publishes on
      success, discards the partial on failure, and sweeps orphaned ones at the next launch.
    -->
    <string name="backup_failed_nothing_saved">No se guardó ningún archivo de copia de seguridad. Los datos de la propia aplicación no se han tocado — la copia de seguridad solo los lee.</string>
    <!--
      The pre-flight failures: the enqueue returned no id, or something between the passphrase recall
      and the enqueue threw. Both are decided before a job exists.
    -->
    <string name="backup_failed_not_started">No se llegó a empezar nada, así que no se guardó ninguna copia de seguridad.</string>
    <!--
      Cancelled without ever running — the chain case, matching restore_cancelled. Every job is appended
      to one chain and WorkManager cancels the dependents of a job that fails, so a backup queued behind
      a failing one is cancelled before its work is ever called. Reported as its own outcome rather than
      as a failure: "Backup failed" over a backup Thor never attempted is a sentence the user cannot act
      on, and the action here is simply to try again.
    -->
    <string name="backup_cancelled">Thor no ha ejecutado esta copia de seguridad — la tarea que iba delante en la cola falló, así que esta se canceló antes de empezar. No se guardó ninguna copia de seguridad. Inténtalo de nuevo.</string>
    <string name="backup_cancelled_after_start">Esta copia de seguridad se canceló cuando ya había empezado.</string>
    <!--
      Its own string rather than restore_interrupted_dismiss, which is named and commented for the §8.5
      breadcrumb banner. Identical text today; rewording one must not silently reword the other.
    -->
    <string name="backup_outcome_dismiss">Entendido</string>
    <!--
      One word, because it is the label the sheet's running frame is built around: while a job is live
      the sheet shows the bar and this button and nothing else. It dismisses the sheet, which is safe
      for the reason the sentence below states — the job is a foreground service and the sheet is only
      watching it.
    -->
    <string name="backup_background">Segundo plano</string>
    <string name="backup_background_desc">Esto sigue en marcha si lo cierras. La notificación muestra cuánto lleva hecho.</string>

    <string name="restore_title">Restaurar una copia de seguridad</string>
    <string name="restore_pick_prompt">Elige un archivo de copia de seguridad de Thor (.thorbak) para ver qué contiene.</string>
    <string name="restore_pick_file">Elegir un archivo</string>
    <string name="restore_pick_another">Elegir otro archivo</string>
    <!--
      PluralsCandidate fires on the %2$d. It is a version code — an identifier the app displays
      alongside the version name — so there is no quantity to agree with and no plural form to pick.
    -->
    <string name="restore_archive_version">Versión %1$s (%2$d)</string>
    <string name="restore_archive_created">Copia creada el %1$s</string>
    <string name="restore_will_install_first">Esta aplicación no está instalada. Thor la instalará primero desde la copia de seguridad.</string>
    <plurals name="restore_include_obb">
        <item quantity="one">Restaurar también los datos del juego (%1$d archivo)</item>
        <item quantity="other">Restaurar también los datos del juego (%1$d archivos)</item>
    </plurals>
    <string name="restore_unlock">Desbloquear</string>
    <string name="restore_unlocked">Desbloqueada con la frase de contraseña guardada.</string>
    <string name="restore_confirm_replace">Entiendo que esto sustituye los datos actuales de la aplicación. Todo lo que no esté en esta copia de seguridad se elimina.</string>
    <string name="restore_start">Restaurar</string>
    <!--
      No `restore_back` any more. The restore surface is a bottom sheet, dismissed by its scrim, its
      drag handle or the back gesture, and its own Background button is what covers leaving a live
      run — so the button this named had nothing left to do. Deleted rather than left in place because
      lint's UnusedResources is fatal in this build; there were no translations to strand.
    -->
    <string name="restore_done">Restauración terminada. Abre la aplicación para comprobar que funciona.</string>
    <!--
      Heading for the sentences a *successful* run still has to report — game data that could not be
      placed, an archive that turned out to hold none, a breadcrumb Thor could not write. The data
      landed, so this is not phrased as a failure: a user told "restore failed" runs it again, which
      deletes and rewrites data that is already correct.
    -->
    <string name="restore_done_warnings">Algunas partes no terminaron:</string>
    <string name="restore_failed">Error en la restauración: %1$s</string>
    <!--
      Its own string rather than backup_failed_unknown, which the backup sheet also renders. Identical
      text today; the two surfaces must be able to diverge without one silently editing the other.
    -->
    <string name="restore_failed_unknown">se detuvo sin decir por qué</string>
    <!--
      The four sentences ArchiveRestoreViewModel used to hold as Kotlin literals. They live here for the
      reason every other string on this screen does: a literal in a view model is invisible to lint and
      to the translation pipeline, so it would have been the only English left once strings_backup.xml
      is translated, with nothing to point at it.

      Two shapes, and the difference is where each is drawn. The two below are whole sentences shown on
      their own — one in the screen's error block, one as the passphrase field's supporting text — so
      they are capitalised and stopped. The two after them are fragments interpolated into
      restore_failed above ("Restore failed: %1$s"), so they carry neither.
    -->
    <string name="restore_error_unreadable_file">Thor no pudo leer ese archivo. Si está en una tarjeta o en una carpeta de la nube, comprueba que siga disponible e inténtalo de nuevo.</string>
    <!--
      Split out of restore_error_unreadable_file, which used to answer for both. The picker shows every
      file on the device, so choosing something that is not a backup is the ordinary mistake, and the
      two failures send the user opposite ways: this one says pick a different file, the other says
      this file is fine, try it again. One sentence for both was wrong for whichever one happened.
    -->
    <string name="restore_error_not_an_archive">Ese archivo no es una copia de seguridad de Thor. Elige un archivo .thorbak.</string>
    <string name="restore_error_wrong_passphrase">Esa frase de contraseña no abre esta copia de seguridad.</string>
    <!--
      Not "wrong passphrase": this is the check itself failing, and a throw is no evidence about what
      was typed. Says what to do instead of guessing at a cause it does not know.
    -->
    <string name="restore_error_unlock_check_failed">Thor no pudo comprobar esa frase de contraseña. Inténtalo de nuevo.</string>
    <string name="restore_failed_passphrase_lost">Thor ya no tiene la frase de contraseña de esta copia de seguridad — desbloquéala otra vez</string>
    <string name="restore_failed_salt_unreadable">no se pudo leer la sal de este archivo</string>
    <!--
      The damage sentence. Shown only where RestoreFinish.workerRan is true — a failed WorkInfo, or a
      cancel this screen saw run — and never as the second line of a failure decided before the
      enqueue. Where it does apply it must never say "nothing was changed": a restore deletes each
      class before it writes it, and on the install-first path it may have installed the app before it
      failed, so the honest instruction is to go and look. Its last sentence tells the user to run a
      destructive operation again, which is the reason the condition on it is load-bearing.
    -->
    <string name="restore_failed_partial">Esto se detuvo a medias, así que los datos de la aplicación pueden estar incompletos — y si Thor instaló la aplicación desde esta copia de seguridad, sigue instalada. Volver a restaurar es la solución.</string>
    <!--
      The pre-flight failures: Thor no longer holds the passphrase, the archive's salt will not decode,
      or the enqueue threw. All three are decided before a job exists, so the device is untouched and
      the string above would be false. Deliberately does not say "try again" — the reason above it says
      what to do, and two instructions in two lines contradict each other.
    -->
    <string name="restore_failed_not_started">No se llegó a empezar nada, así que no ha cambiado nada en este dispositivo.</string>
    <!--
      Cancelled without ever running: jobs share one chain, WorkManager cancels the dependents of a job
      that fails, and a dependent's work is never called. This is the only cancel string that gets to
      say "nothing was changed", and it is true only because of that. (restore_failed_not_started above
      says the same of a failure decided before the enqueue.) The sibling below covers a cancel that
      arrived after the worker had started.
    -->
    <string name="restore_cancelled">Thor no ha ejecutado esta restauración — la tarea que iba delante en la cola falló, así que esta se canceló antes de empezar. No se cambió nada. Inténtalo de nuevo.</string>
    <!--
      Cancelled after the job had been started, which this screen knows because it observed the RUNNING
      state — written as WorkManager hands the job to a built worker. Rendered above
      restore_failed_partial: the outcome differs from a failure, the damage does not.
    -->
    <string name="restore_cancelled_after_start">Esta restauración se canceló cuando ya había empezado.</string>
    <string name="restore_interrupted">La restauración de %1$s no terminó. Los datos de esa aplicación pueden estar incompletos — volver a restaurarla es la solución.</string>
    <string name="restore_interrupted_dismiss">Entendido</string>
    <!--
      The outcome card's dismiss, split out from restore_interrupted_dismiss above. That one belongs to
      the §8.5 breadcrumb banner, which is what its name and its neighbours say; the outcome card had
      borrowed it. Same text, two owners, so either can be reworded on its own.
    -->
    <string name="restore_outcome_dismiss">Entendido</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_signer_mismatch">Esta copia de seguridad se hizo desde una aplicación firmada por otro desarrollador. Restaurarla entregaría a una aplicación los datos de otra, así que Thor no lo hará.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_signer_unverifiable">Thor no pudo leer la firma de la aplicación instalada, así que no puede comprobar que esta copia de seguridad le pertenezca.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_app_absent">Esta aplicación no está instalada y esta copia de seguridad no contiene ningún instalador con el que añadirla.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_class_missing">Esta copia de seguridad no contiene uno de los elementos seleccionados.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_nothing_selected">No hay nada seleccionado.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_schema_too_new">Esta copia de seguridad la hizo una versión más reciente de Thor. Actualiza Thor para restaurarla.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_invalid_schema_version">Esta copia de seguridad no indica en qué formato está. El archivo está dañado, o no lo escribió Thor.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_invalid_package_name">Esta copia de seguridad nombra una aplicación de una forma que Thor no acepta. El archivo está dañado, o no lo escribió Thor.</string>
    <string name="restore_refused_invalid_user_id">Esta copia de seguridad nombra un perfil de usuario que Thor no acepta. El archivo está dañado, o no lo escribió Thor.</string>
    <string name="restore_warning_version_older">La aplicación instalada es más antigua que esta copia de seguridad. Una aplicación puede fallar con datos de una versión más reciente — si pasa, actualiza antes la aplicación.</string>
    <string name="restore_warning_ce_without_de">Esta copia de seguridad contiene datos de arranque, pero no están seleccionados. Algunas aplicaciones necesitan los dos y pueden comportarse como si estuvieran a medio migrar.</string>
    <string name="restore_settings_desc">Lee un archivo .thorbak y devuelve los datos de una aplicación a su sitio.</string>
    <!--
      The Settings section heading, covering both halves: the row under it restores, and Task 18 adds
      the saved-passphrase row to the same section. Not action_backup ("Back up"), which is a button
      label on an app — a heading that reads as a verb over a row that reads as a different verb.
    -->
    <string name="backup_and_restore">Copia de seguridad y restauración</string>

    <!--
      §5.4's surface. Separate strings from the backup sheet's rather than shared ones: the sheet is
      talking about the archive it is about to write ("this backup"), and this screen is talking about
      a device-wide setting that applies to every future one. One string cannot be true in both places
      without going vague in both.
    -->
    <string name="passphrase_settings_title">Frase de contraseña de las copias</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_desc">Elige, cambia o quita la frase de contraseña que Thor recuerda.</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_stored">Hay una frase de contraseña guardada en este dispositivo.</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_warning">Thor no puede recuperar esta frase de contraseña. Sin ella, una copia de seguridad hecha con ella no se puede restaurar.</string>
    <!--
      The consequence a user cannot see and would otherwise assume the opposite of. Changing a stored
      passphrase re-encrypts nothing: each archive's key is derived from the passphrase it was written
      with, so every existing .thorbak still opens only with that one. Stated where the choice is made,
      because after it is made there is nothing to be done about it.
    -->
    <string name="passphrase_settings_no_reencrypt">Cambiarla no cambia ninguna copia de seguridad que ya hayas hecho. Cada una sigue abriéndose solo con la frase de contraseña con la que se hizo.</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_save">Guardar</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_forget">Olvidarla</string>
    <string name="passphrase_settings_saved">Guardada en este dispositivo.</string>
    <!--
      The confirmation body has to carry the scope of the loss, because the button label cannot: what
      goes is the ability to open every .thorbak already written under that passphrase, not merely the
      copy this device is holding. The consequence is then spelled out as something the user will have
      to do (type it again) rather than left at "it cannot be recovered", so a user who does still know
      their passphrase can see this is survivable and proceed.
    -->
    <string name="passphrase_forget_confirm_title">¿Olvidar esta frase de contraseña?</string>
    <string name="passphrase_forget_confirm_desc">Thor dejará de recordarla en este dispositivo. No se puede recuperar — tendrás que escribirla otra vez para cada copia de seguridad que hagas y para cada copia de seguridad que ya hayas hecho con ella.</string>
    <!--
      Load-bearing, not decorative: without it lint fails the build with
      `Error: Formatting %d followed by words ("characters") … [PluralsCandidate]`.

      Kept because the argument is always MIN_PASSPHRASE_LENGTH, a compile-time constant — so this
      string renders one number and only ever that number, and no plural form can vary. It is NOT
      kept on the grounds that "characters" is uncounted: lint names that noun and it is right to,
      and values-ar (on the translation list, where 8 takes a different form from 1 and 2) would
      otherwise be a real problem. Do not lean on restore_archive_version's precedent here; that one
      is exempt because a version code is not a count at all, which is a different argument.

      If MIN_PASSPHRASE_LENGTH ever becomes user-configurable or varies by build, this reasoning
      expires with it and the string becomes a genuine <plurals>.
    -->
    <string name="passphrase_error_too_short">Usa al menos %1$d caracteres.</string>
    <string name="passphrase_error_mismatch">Las dos frases de contraseña no coinciden.</string>
    <string name="passphrase_error_store_failed">Thor no pudo guardar la frase de contraseña en este dispositivo. La copia de seguridad funcionará igualmente — solo tendrás que escribirla cada vez.</string>

